  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/262e1b0c4e0ddf338c527b487fcadc85baac12712f91cb4c54b771e49add25b9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-{x}^{2}\\,\u27f9\\,x=\\sqrt{4-y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/48c874b069b55599f76a8fe1aa50c5f4db36ff8943d264a107452668d689174b.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2(4-y){e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{8(4)-{4}^{2}}-{e}^{8(0)-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
